deep.reconstruction:
Context: in the notes on the 'structure at infinity' of the moduli stacks M_{g,ν}, Grothendieck wants a local, combinatorial model for how a space with a normal-crossings boundary divisor is reassembled from 'elementary' pieces. These pieces are global strata, tubular germs and deleted tubular germs.

One-dimensional case (previous page). There are four elementary pieces (264):
- C^*, the open stratum (global);
- P = {0}, the pointed stratum (global);
- D, the germ of disc at 0 (local);
- D^*, the germ of punctured disc (local).
They are linked by the inclusion diagram (265): P ⊂ D ⊃ D^* ⊂ C^*. The pair (C, divisor at 0) is recovered as the amalgamated sum (266) C ≅ C^* ⊔_{D^*} D. Read homotopically or topos-theoretically, this is a gluing (homotopy pushout / Artin-type gluing) of the open part with a tubular neighbourhood along the deleted neighbourhood.

This page (267): the n-dimensional local model is X = C^I, with I a finite set of cardinality n, and Θ = div(∏_{i∈I} z_i) the coordinate-hyperplane normal-crossings divisor. Grothendieck expects:
- the 'elementary types' (elementary topoi) for this situation to be the I-indexed products of the four one-dimensional types;
- the canonical inclusions among these product types to be induced factorwise from diagram (265).
In other words, the indexing category is essentially (diagram 265)^I, and X should be the corresponding iterated or cubical amalgam.

Elementary types are therefore classified by functions I → {C^*, P, D, D^*}. Equivalently they are classified by 'generalized partitions' of I into four labelled classes, where empty classes are allowed (marginal note). He organises these as (268):
- I = I' ⊔ I'', where I' indexes the global factors and I'' the local factors;
- I' = I'_0 ⊔ I'_1, where I'_0 indexes the P factors (dim 0) and I'_1 the dimension-1 global factors (which must be C^*);
- I'' = I''_0 ⊔ I''^*, where I''_0 indexes the D factors and I''^* the D^* factors.
There are 4^n elementary types.

The next page extracts the invariants:
- the codimension d'_0 = card I'_0;
- the dimension d_1 = card I'_1 of the 'soul' (âme) C^{*I'_1} × P^{I'_0};
- the strata D_J = P^J × C^{I∖J} and the open strata D^*_J = P^J × C^{*I∖J}.
So each elementary topos is a stratum ('soul') together with its normal data: tubular germ factors and deleted-tubular germ factors.

Purpose: to build the 'topos (or homotopy type) at infinity' of M_{g,ν}, near the Deligne–Mumford boundary, by gluing such standard local pieces. This is the analytic or topological counterpart of describing the boundary via tangential base points or deleted tubular neighbourhoods.
